TEXT 64 32 Left 2 ;Non-inverting Schmitt trigger
TEXT 64 656 Left 2 !.tran 0 20m 0 2u
TEXT 64 704 Left 2 ;Plot V(VIN) and V(VOUT). Positive feedback creates hysteresis.
TEXT 64 768 Left 2 ;1. Click Run: the running-person button at the top left.
TEXT 64 808 Left 2 ;2. On the schematic, click the wire labeled VOUT (not the component).
TEXT 64 848 Left 2 ;3. Click the VIN wire too, to compare input and output in the same plot.
TEXT 64 888 Left 2 ;If the plot is empty, select the schematic window and click the labeled wire again.
TEXT 64 968 Left 2 ;EXPERIMENTS AND COMMON QUESTIONS
TEXT 64 1008 Left 2 ;Right-click a component to edit its value. Predict first, change one value, then Run.
TEXT 64 1048 Left 2 ;PLOT: VIN, VOUT, then VSUM (the positive input). The minus input is 0 V.
TEXT 64 1088 Left 2 ;When VSUM crosses 0 V, VOUT switches and feedback reinforces the change.
TEXT 64 1128 Left 2 ;With VOUT near -5 V, VIN must rise to about +1.06 V to switch high.
TEXT 64 1168 Left 2 ;With VOUT near +5 V, VIN must fall to about -1.06 V to switch low.
TEXT 64 1208 Left 2 ;The output is a two-level waveform, not an amplified sine wave.
TEXT 64 1248 Left 2 ;TRY: Change RF from 47k to 100k. The hysteresis window gets narrower.
TEXT 64 1288 Left 2 ;Thresholds are approximately +/-|VOUT|*RIN/RF, not exactly the supply rails.
